course: dictionary
lesson: 3355
title: Praising good work: excellently
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Excellently» is an adverb. An adverb tells you how, when or where something happens. In «You have managed excellently», the word tells you how well you managed.
explain: «Excellently» means extremely well, in a way that is of the very best quality. Teachers use it to praise good work, as in «You have done excellently».
explain: «Excellently» is less common than «very well», so it sounds a little formal, like a teacher writing a report. You can use it when you want to praise someone properly.
fact: «Excellently» is an adverb, so it describes a verb such as managed, done or played.
fact: «Excellently» means extremely well, in a way of the very best quality.
fact: Its base word is «excellent», an adjective: «an excellent painting».
fact: Teachers often write «You have done excellently» in school reports.
fact: In everyday talk you might say «very well»; «excellently» sounds more formal.
exercise: In «You have managed excellently», what does excellently describe? | The name of a person | *The way you managed | The time of day | The colour of the sky
exercise: Ella's teacher writes, «Your model was built excellently.» What does excellently mean here? | *Extremely well, of the very best quality | Quickly and carelessly | Very loudly | Late in the day
exercise: Which word would a teacher most likely write in a report to praise good work? | grumpily | cheekily | *excellently | sleepily
test: Maya sang excellently at the concert. What does excellently describe? | *How Maya sang | When the concert happened | Where the concert was held | How many people came
test: Which phrase is the most formal way to praise someone's work? | Nah, that was rubbish | You did it, cool | *You have done excellently | Whatever, it's fine
exam: Mr Patel writes, «Ben has worked excellently this term.» What does excellently tell you? | Ben has worked in the park | *Ben has worked extremely well, to the best standard | Ben has worked very slowly | Ben has not worked at all
